# Code review of Pair A, by Pair C

Reviewers: Pair C · Internal cross-review,
Files read: TD2/Pair A/vote-quorum.py, TD2/Pair A/TD2_groupe_A.md

## What we checked
- vote-quorum.py reuses vote_k_sur_n from the supplied skeleton instead of rewriting it.
- Both thresholds respect a strict majority, 2 of 3 and 3 of 5.
- Only 'real' rows are used, and a member in error (HTTP 429, invalid or rejected SQL) stays in the panel without voting.
- The reference is read only after the decision, so the vote cannot be steered by the expected value.

## Link with the Pair C routing rule
- C15, C17 and C19 are combination questions. The 2-of-3 panel abstains on all three, because only GPT-OSS 120B is correct there and Cohere and Qwen disagree with each other. This supports our choice not to rely on that panel for combinations. Our rule uses GPT-OSS 20B and 120B, both 5/5 on this family in TD 1, plus a reference check when the figure is shown to a customer.
- C02 moves from consensus (2 of 3) to abstention (3 of 5). Adding two models that are wrong on this question costs the consensus. A larger panel is not automatically safer, it depends on which models it adds. This matches our F2 rule, where GPT-OSS 20B (3/4) is not called alone.
- On C15 and C17, the two agreeing votes in the 3-of-5 quorum both come from the GPT-OSS family. A quorum built on one family limits the protection against shared errors.

## Points raised
- "Last attempt" is the last row read for each model. This is correct only if traces-td1.csv is in chronological order. To confirm on C18 and C19 of GPT-OSS 20B, which have an HTTP 429 before a success.


